  "slug": "ai-topic-c4ec",
  "summary": "Interpretability and benchmarking studies now test whether sequence-based models encode biological mechanism beyond predictive accuracy.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is whether sequence models learn biology that is interpretable and mechanistically grounded, rather than only scoring well on benchmarks. BEND 2.0 extends DNA language model evaluation from variant effect prediction to sequence design and reports gains from linear readouts and post-training. CNN-CR, an interpretable neural network, recovers compact shared promoter motif families that predict expression across 116 human biosamples, with motif interpretation differing across cell types. A splicing study finds that black-box neural splice-site predictors depend on reading-frame-altering mutations, information unavailable to the splicing machinery, and that they miss poison exons, whereas grey-box models behave differently. The new element is diagnostic scrutiny of what models use, not only how accurately they predict.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-06"
}
